Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Final Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  OPERATION DATE  15 REMOVED_MONTH 97 ACCESSIONED ON  15 REMOVED_MONTH 97 at 16 14 CLINICAL DATA   None Provided                                                                                      FINAL DIAGNOSIS     BREAST  LEFT   SIMPLE MASTECTOMY  INVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA  GRADE 2  REMOVED_DATE CM  OCCUPYING THE WALL OF THE BIOPSY CAVITY AND INVADING LYMPHATIC VESSELS  DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU  EXTENSIVE  EXTENDING TO THE INFERIOR MARGIN OF THE SPECIMEN  HEALING BIOPSY CAVITY  SEE NOTE  NOTE  Blood vessel invasion is not seen  The margin of the specimen appears free of invasive carcinoma  Diagnosis by  Frederick Carl Koerner  M D  Signed On  21 REMOVED_MONTH 97 at 07 45                                                                                     RECEPTOR IMMUNOHISTOCHEMISTRY Addendum     RESULTS OF STAINING OF FIXED TISSUE FOR HORMONE RECEPTOR PROTEINS BREAST  LEFT   The tumor cells stain for estrogen receptor protein  The tumor cells stain for progesterone receptor protein  See note  Note  In this laboratory  we used this antibody to stain 100 breast carcinomas prepared in the usual way  paraffin sections  and we compared the results with determinations of estrogen receptor by biochemical assay  91  of tumors that stained with this antibody were positive for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  96  of tumors that failed to stain with this antibody were negative for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  When we compared the results of staining of paraffin sections with the results of staining of frozen sections  the predictive value of a positive result was 97  and the predictive value of a negative result was 95   In this laboratory  we also compared the results of staining of 100 routinely processed breast carcinomas with determinations of progesterone receptors by a hormone binding assay  Sixty five percent of tumors that stained with this antibody contained progesterone receptors by the biochemical assay  and 98  of tumors that failed to stain lacked the receptors  Added by  Frederick Carl Koerner  M D  Signed On  25 REMOVED_MONTH 97 at 14 30 GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received fresh  labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  s name and unit number and  left breast  is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 3 0cm simple mastectomy specimen overlaid by a REMOVED_DATE x 2 5cm areola entered by a 0 8cm in maximal diameter nipple  The nipple surface is slightly irregular and the nipple appears slightly retracted  The specimen superior half  designated by the surgeon with a short stitch  is inked black  the inferior half is inked blue and the deep resection margin is inked green  The lateral resection margin  designated by the surgeon with a long stitch  is inked yellow  Along the inferior border of the mastectomy is a REMOVED_DATE x 0 4cm skin ellipse  centered by a 2 8cm long  well healed linear scar  Sectioning deeper in this area reveals a REMOVED_DATE x 0 6cm biopsy cavity  that extends for an approximate length of 3 5cm  along the inferior resection margin of the specimen  From this area radiate numerous white fibrous bands that reach the resection margin in multiple points  Superior to the biopsy site is an irregular REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 1 5cm area of white  slightly firm breast parenchyma  A REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 1 5cm white  firm area is located in the superolateral portion of the breast  scattered with yellow granules  measuring 0 2cm in maximal diameter  No lymph nodes can be dissected from the superolateral portion of the specimen  Section Code  A D  Biopsy cavity  E  Central area of firmness  F G  Superolateral area area of slight nodularity H  Nipple  Resident  EDI BROGI  MD  PhD                                                                                 SOURCE CARE UNIT  MGH INPATIENT  FLR UNKNOWN  REPORTS TO  Barbara Lynn Smith  M D  Michael J Yaremchuk  M D  Michael L Grossbard  M D  Edi Brogi  M D   PhD  PART   BREAST  LEFT   SIMPLE MASTECTOMY   report_end  , Unchecked.
